steps.body:
`<algorithm>` and `<numeric>` also work on a whole vector at once. You pass `v.begin(), v.end()`, which means "from the first element to the end". The Iterators lesson explains what these are.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {5, 2, 9, 2, 7};
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());                                // ascending, in place
std::reverse(v.begin(), v.end());                             // flip the order
std::cout << std::count(v.begin(), v.end(), 2) << '\n';       // how many 2s
std::cout << *std::max_element(v.begin(), v.end()) << '\n';   // largest value
std::cout << std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0) << '\n';  // sum, from <numeric>
```

`std::max_element` and `std::min_element` return the *position* of the value, and the `*` in front reads the value there. Never use them on an empty vector, because there is no element to read.

`std::accumulate` adds onto its start value, and **the start value sets the result type**. Start from `0` for an `int` sum, `0LL` when it may pass 2 billion, and `0.0` for doubles or an average. With `0`, every partial sum is an `int`, so doubles get truncated.

`std::iota(v.begin(), v.end(), 1)` fills 1, 2, 3, and so on, and `std::fill(v.begin(), v.end(), 0)` sets every element to 0. `<numeric>` also has `std::partial_sum`, the library version of the running totals you wrote by hand.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <numeric>
#include <vector>

void printVector(std::vector<int> values) {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < values.size(); ++i) {
        if (i > 0) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << values[i];
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {5, 2, 9, 2, 7};

    const int sum = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);
    const double average = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0.0) / v.size();
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2);
    std::cout << sum << ' ' << average << '\n';
    std::cout << std::count(v.begin(), v.end(), 2) << '\n';
    std::cout << *std::max_element(v.begin(), v.end()) << ' '
              << *std::min_element(v.begin(), v.end()) << '\n';

    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    printVector(v);
    std::reverse(v.begin(), v.end());
    printVector(v);

    std::vector<int> ids(5);
    std::iota(ids.begin(), ids.end(), 1);
    printVector(ids);
    std::fill(ids.begin(), ids.end(), 0);
    printVector(ids);
    return 0;
}

